  "step_2": "The individual reports having looked at Lottery A first, then Lottery B. The key consideration mentioned is that 'the chance was higher to win something than in the other lottery'. This indicates that the individual focused on the probability of winning a non-zero amount (i.e., the chance of a favorable outcome). The justification centers on comparing the likelihood of receiving any positive amount, not on the magnitude of the prize or expected value. The individual does not mention expected value, variance, regret, or other criteria—only the chance of winning something.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on comparing the skewness of the lotteries via the probability assigned to favorable (non-zero) outcomes. The individual considers exactly this: the higher chance of winning something (i.e., a non-zero amount). Lottery A has a 90% chance of a positive outcome, while B has only 45%, so A is more left-skewed and preferred by the reason. The individual’s focus on the higher chance of winning something aligns directly with the informational basis of the decision reason. Furthermore, the justification (higher chance to win something) matches the rule of preferring the more left-skewed lottery. There is strong alignment between the information used and the reasoning process described. Therefore, it is highly likely the individual used the skewness-based reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 100
